Z||000001||terryk||01-16-2001||12:40 AM||||I used Scotch 77, but 74 is good too. Just hit any upholstery shop or fabric store. They will know immediately what to suggest.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||01-16-2001||12:45 AM||||try this link for some good info: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=4me6mt3k&Catid=18&Id=37&Action=Edit" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.mn.org/Knowledge/Procedure.asp?Version=4me6mt3k&Catid=18&Id=37&Action=Edit</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||zeusrekning||01-16-2001||02:16 AM||||i bought a can from an interior(headliner) shop.It was $10 a can but works great.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||01-16-2001||06:42 AM||||I always use Formica contact cement in a spray paint gun. Unlike all the spray cans Ive used for last 20 years, it wont fall down after a few days in the sun. Ive used 3M too, use it now for sticking pics on my garage wall. Any hardware store sells contact cement from pint cans up for a few dollars.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||01-16-2001||08:34 AM||||I use 3M Foamfast74 with good results.<P>Did you clean all the old foam remnants from the headliner board and spray glue on both the headliner board and the fabric surfaces?<P>I'm surprised that the foam backed fabric glue did not work.<P>The Foamfast74 sprays on like silly string and acts like a slow setting contact cement.  Once you put the two surfaces together you have about 10 minutes to work out the wrinkles before the glue sets up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||01-16-2001||10:33 AM||||I went to a upolstery shop with a quart jar and had him fill it with the contact cement he uses on headliners. $5.00||||reg||1
